\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces The point-to-sentence conversion at \textcircled {\scriptsize  4} DRAFT, shown both ways round. Forward: one approved outline POINT (\texttt  {C3.P1.B4}) becomes one or more sentence scaffolds (\texttt  {C3.P1.S1}--\texttt  {S3}), each hole sitting exactly where its answer will sit, and DRAFT may never invent the value, citation, reading or figure. Backward: the machine-readable \texttt  {realizes:} comment on each scaffold line recovers the point from any sentence, so REVISE can split or merge sentences without losing the join. The shared \texttt  {C3.P1} prefix is the join key; \texttt  {B} (a point in the plan) is owned by haipipe-plugin-outline, \texttt  {S} (a sentence on the page) by haipipe-sentence, and neither redefines the other.}}{1}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:qpw00-point-to-sentence}{{1}{1}{}{figure.1}{}}
\gdef \@abspage@last{1}
